 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/De1lAfvy1a2/",
 "title": "Prompt vs Skill in AI Interviews",
 "topic": "ai tools",
 "summary": "This video explains the difference between a prompt and an AI skill for interview preparation. The creator defines a prompt as instructions given once in a chat, whereas a skill is a saved folder of instructions and scripts that the AI loads automatically only when needed.",
 "key_points": [
  "A skill is essentially a prompt saved in a file called skill.md instead of being typed into the chat.",
  "System prompts get sent in full with every single message, which wastes tokens even when the instructions aren't needed.",
  "A smarter approach is to turn repeating tasks into skills so they stay out of the way until needed.",
  "Every skill.md starts with a tiny label containing a name and description so the model can match your request to it.",
  "A skill can also be a folder containing skill.md, a report template, and a small Python script (like check.py) to run and verify numbers.",
  "Use a prompt for a one-off question and a skill for repeating tasks."
 ],
 "content": "Both product manager and engineering aspirants will face this question: what’s the difference between a skill and a prompt? Here’s the correct answer👆 Make a note of this post. If an interviewer asks you the difference between a prompt and a skill, the simple answer is that a skill is actually also a prompt. It's just instructions saved in a file called skill.md instead of typed up into the chat. So if both end up as text that the model reads, why do we need skills at all? Because of a problem almost all of us run into. We use AI for the same few tasks again and again. And every time we type the same prompts on ChatGPT, say to build a report for clients, a smart person fixes this repetition by putting those instructions into the system prompt, so they never have to type them again. That works until you realize that the system prompt is sent in full with every message whether it's needed or not. So even when you ask for a quick email, the model is still reading your report rules. A smarter person turns each of those tasks into a skill because a skill stays out of the way until it's needed. But then how does the model know when it's needed? At the top of every skill.md, there is a name and a short description like use this when writing the weekly client report. When a chat starts, the app loads only those few lines from every skill. When you ask for the report, the model matches your request with that description and only then opens the full instructions. You can also call the skill by a name. Now, the smartest people go one step further because a skill doesn't just have to be one file. It's a folder, and skill.md is only the first file in it. Next to it, they keep the report template which the model opens only when the instructions point to it, and a small script that checks the numbers in the report add up. So the model runs the script instead of reading it, so only the result comes into the chat and the check works the same way every week. So use a prompt for a one-off question and a skill for every task you keep repeating. So the answer for the interviewer is, a prompt is the instructions you give once, and a skill is a saved folder of instructions and scripts that the AI loads by itself only when the task's needed. Comment skill and I'll send over my notes on how to create a skill.",
